The **Determinantal Conjecture** (also known as the Marcus–de Oliveira conjecture) is a famous **open problem** in linear algebra / matrix theory, dating back to the 1970s. The Lean formalization itself marks it as `@[category research open, AMS 15]`, confirming it is an unsolved research problem.

The conjecture asserts that for two n×n normal complex matrices A and B, the determinant det(A+B) lies in the convex hull of the n! points ∏ᵢ (λᵢ(A) + λ_σ(i)(B)) as σ ranges over the symmetric group Sₙ.

This problem has resisted solution for decades. While it has been verified in special cases (e.g., when one matrix is Hermitian, when n ≤ 3, when the matrices commute, when one is positive semidefinite), the general case remains open. The key difficulties include:

1. The eigenvalues of a sum of normal matrices do not relate simply to the eigenvalues of the summands (unlike the Hermitian case with Weyl's inequalities).
2. The convex-hull structure is delicate and no known technique (majorization, characteristic polynomial manipulations, variational characterizations) has been able to pin down det(A+B) within this polytope in general.
3. Even partial progress requires deep interplay between the spectral theory of normal matrices and the geometry of the determinant map.

I do not have a proof of this conjecture, and producing one would constitute a major research breakthrough.

